          "id": "satoplam-t08-q001",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q1",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q1",
          "passage": "12.5sec. (*Exam level)\nWhen people think of dinosaurs with feathers,\nthey typically think of winged dinosaurs, such as\nthe bat-like Ambopteryx. However, many\ndinosaurs that didn't have wings also had\nfeathers on their bodies. For instance, research\nindicates that the wingless large Yutyrannus\nlikely had feathers.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To discuss the presence of feathers on\ncertain types of dinosaurs.",
            "To point out the differences between\ndinosaur feathers and bird feathers.",
            "To explain why feathered dinosaurs went\nextinct.",
            "To argue that only one species of dinosaur\nhad feathers."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q1). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q002",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q2",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q2",
          "passage": "Smartwatches and fitness trackers currently\navailable to the public claim to be able to\nprovide data on VO2 max, a measurement of an\nindividual's fitness level that indicates heart\ndisease and mortality risk. However, whether or\nnot these measurements are accurate is still\nunproven. Meanwhile, Cambridge developed a\nmodel that can provide more accurate tracking\nof one's heart rate and can also monitor fitness\nchanges leading to better indications of health\nrisks.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To prove that currently available\nsmartwatches are not an accurate\nindicator of overall health.",
            "To introduce a new fitness tracker model\nthat may provide more accurate data on\nhealth than current products.",
            "To argue that individuals should not rely\non fitness trackers or smartwatches to\nmonitor their health.",
            "To summarize the findings of a new fitness\ntracker model that will be available to the\npublic soon."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q2). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q003",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q3",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q3",
          "passage": "Researchers have found what they believe to be\na cause behind mice being motivated to exercise:\ngut microbes. The specific molecules in these\nmicrobes appear to stimulate the desire in the\nbrain to run and continue running. It has been\npostulated that if this bacteria could be given to\npeople in pill form, they too could experience\nthe same motivation to exercise.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To understand better what makes mice\nmotivated to move.",
            "To suggest that the findings in mice could\nbe used to increase human activity.",
            "To explain research in mice that will be\nused to support people.",
            "To imply that a lack of motivation to work\nout is not controllable."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q3). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q004",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q4",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q4",
          "passage": "Eden Robinson is a novelist and a member of\nthe Haisia Nation in western Canada. Critics\nand fellow writers have praised her work for\ncombining traditional Haisia stories with\npopular genres of literature, such as fantasy and\nmystery fiction. But Robinson is not the only\nIndigenous writer to blend traditional stories\nwith popular literature. In the 2019 novel\nEmpire of wild, Cherie Dimaline successfully\nblended the oral storytelling tradition of her\npeople, The Metis, with horror fiction.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To discuss two writers who blend\ntraditional indigenous stories with popular\ngenres.",
            "To urge younger Indigenous authors to\navoid writing in popular genres.",
            "To argue that traditional Indigenous\nstories are more memorable than most\nnovels are.",
            "To explain why one indigenous author has\nachieved more success than another has."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q4). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q005",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q5",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q5",
          "passage": "Luang Prabang has high pedestrian traffic, but\nsimply replicating a feature of Luang Prabang\nassociated with walkability—e.g., its\nhuman-scaled architecture—may be insufficient\nto induce increased walking in other cities. As\nurbanist Mariela Alfonzo argues, our\nunderstanding of individuals' decision-making\nabout whether to walk is insufficiently robust:\nsome studies emphasize the role of local norms,\nothers the role of demographic characteristics,\nand so on, but walking decisions are made in\ncomplex context in which multiple conditions\nand needs inform individuals' choices.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To describe the effect of human-scaled\narchitecture on any given city's walkability.",
            "To explain why it is challenging to\ncompare the amount of pedestrian traffic\nin different cities.",
            "To present a claim about how individuals'\ndecision-making about walking can be\nimproved.",
            "To discuss the difficulty of identifying a\nreliable way to increase walking in any\ngiven city."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q5). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q006",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q6",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q6",
          "passage": "Researchers have long hypothesized that woolly\nmammoths were hunted to extinction in North\nAmerica by humans using spears with grooved\ntips known as Clovis points. One anthropologist\nset out to test this hypothesis. Using a\nmechanical spear-thrower, he launched spears\nwith Clovis points into mounds of\nclay—substitutes for the animals' large bodies.\nThe projectiles generally penetrated only a few\ninches into the clay, an amount insufficient to\nhave harmed most woolly mammoths. This led\nthe anthropologist to conclude that hunters\nusing spears with Clovis points likely weren't\nthe principal drivers of the extinction.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To argue for the significance of new\nfindings amid an ongoing debate among\nresearchers.",
            "To discuss the advantages and\ndisadvantages of the method used in an\nexperiment.",
            "To summarize two competing hypotheses\nand a major finding associated with each\none.",
            "To describe an experiment whose results\ncast doubt on an established hypothesis."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q6). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q007",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q7",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q7",
          "passage": "Scholarly interest in literary juvenilia—writings\nby children and teenagers—tends to focus on\nunpublished works by authors who became\nfamous as adults, such as Charles Dickens's\npoem \"The Bill of Fare,\" which he wrote around\nthe ages of 18-20, because they offer insights\ninto their authors' artistic development. But\nsome scholars also argue that recovering\njuvenilia by lesser-known writers is essential to\nunderstanding literary history: Daisy Ashford's\nnovels, which she published as a child, were\nwidely read by contemporaries and are therefore\ndeserving of closer attention.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To describe the challenges famous writers\nencountered when seeking to publish works\nwritten in their childhood.",
            "To present reasons why literary scholars\nconsider juvenilia to be valuable resources.",
            "To compare the accomplishments of young\nwriters with those of their adult\ncontemporaries.",
            "To argue that Ashford's novels have more\nliterary merit than Dickens's juvenilia do."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q7). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q008",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q8",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q8",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Jean\nWebster's 1912 novel Daddy-Long-Legs. The\nnarrator is a young college student writing\nletters detailing her weekly experiences.\n(The college is) organizing the Freshman\nbasketball team and there's just a chance that I\nshall make it. I'm little of course, but terribly\nquick and wiry and tough. While the others are\nhopping about in the air, I can dodge under\ntheir feet and grab the ball.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To compare basketball with other sports.",
            "To provide details of how to play\nbasketball.",
            "To state how players will be chosen for the\nbasketball team.",
            "To explain why the narrator thinks she\nmight make the basketball team."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q8). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q009",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q9",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q9",
          "passage": "In the late 1800s, Spanish-language newspapers\nflourished in cities across Texas. San Antonio\nalone produced eleven newspapers in Spanish\nbetween 1890 and 1900. But El Paso surpassed\nall other cities in the state. This city produced\ntwenty-two newspapers in Spanish during that\nperiod. El Paso is located on the border with\nMexico and has always had a large population of\nSpanish speakers. Thus, it is unsurprising that\nthis city became such a rich site for\nSpanish-language journalism.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To compare Spanish-language newspapers\npublished in Texas today with ones\npublished there during the late 1800s.",
            "To explain that Spanish-language\nnewspapers thrived in Texas and especially\nin El Paso during the late 1800s.",
            "To argue that Spanish-language\nnewspapers published in El Paso\ninfluenced the ones published in San\nAntonio during the late 1800s.",
            "To explain why Spanish-language\nnewspapers published in Texas were so\npopular in Mexico during the late 1800s."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q9). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q010",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q10",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q10",
          "passage": "The following text is from Betty Smith's 1943\nnovel A Tree Grows in Brooklyn. Francie, a\nyoung girl, visits the library often.\nFrancie thought that all the books in the world\nwere in that library and she had a plan about\nreading all the books in the world. She was\nreading a book a day in alphabetical order and\nnot skipping the dry ones. She remembered that\nthe first author had been Abbott. She had been\nreading a book a day for a long time now and\nshe was still in the B's. Already she had read\nabout bees and buffaloes, Bermuda vacations\nand Byzantine architecture. For all her\nenthusiasm, she had to admit that some of the\nB's had been hard going. But Francie was a\nreader.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To illustrate Francie's enjoyment of an\nunusual topic.",
            "To explain why Francie prefers reading\nover other activities.",
            "To portray Francie's determination to\nmeet a goal.",
            "To describe a book that Francie greatly\nadmires."
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is C (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q10). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q011",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q11",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q11",
          "passage": "Gus Edwards's atmospheric play Black Body\nBlues was produced in 1978 by the\ngroundbreaking Negro Ensemble Company\n(NEC). NEC cofounder Douglas Turner Ward,\nwho worked as an actor, director, and\nplaywright, had met actor and producer Robert\nHooks while they were performing in a 1960\ntouring production of Lorraine Hansberry's play\nA Raisin in the Sun. Together, they envisioned\na theater company that would nurture and\nshowcase the work of Black theater\nprofessionals. Since NEC's founding in 1967, its\nworkshops and performances have given Black\nplaywrights, including Edwards, a forum for\ntheir compelling stories.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To show how the play A Raisin in the Sun\ninfluenced Ward and Hooks.",
            "To bring attention to a work by Gus\nEdwards.",
            "To describe how Ward and Hooks's\ncreative partnership changed over time.",
            "To discuss the origin and importance of\nthe NEC."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q11). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q012",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q12",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q12",
          "passage": "Today composer Scott Joplin is mainly\ncelebrated for his catchy ragtime pieces \"Maple\nLeaf Rag\" and \"The Entertainer.\" However, by\noverlooking his less famous works, listeners will\nmiss the full range of Joplin's creativity. For\ninstance, his waltz \"Pleasant Moments\" and his\nopera \"Treemonisha\" skillfully blend ragtime\nand classical music. These masterpieces deserve\nas much fame as Joplin's biggest hits.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To argue that more attention should be\ngiven to Joplin's lesser-known works.",
            "To describe the similarities and differences\nbetween ragtime music and opera.",
            "To explain how Joplin learned to compose\nand perform ragtime music.",
            "To encourage music lovers to listen to\nmusic by many different composers."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q12). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q013",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q13",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q13",
          "passage": "Desolation Island, first published in 1978, is a\nnovel in Patrick O'Brian's Aubrey/Maturin\nseries, which includes twenty completed books.\nSome critics have found fault with the abrupt\nendings of Desolation Island and other books in\nthe series, saying that they do not finish\nconclusively but arbitrarily stop. But other\ncritics argue that the books should not be\nthought of as discrete texts with traditional\nbeginnings and endings but as a single incredibly\nlong work, similar to other multivolume stories,\nsuch as John Galsworthy's The Forsyte Saga.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To explain why many critics find the\nAubrey/Maturin novels to be remarkably\nentertaining despite flaws in the novels'\nstructures.",
            "To describe a characteristic of the\nAubrey/Maturin novels and offer two\ndiffering viewpoints on this characteristic.",
            "To argue that the Aubrey/Maturin series\nshould have the literary renown of The\nForsyte Saga.",
            "To present a reason the unusual structure\nthat O'Brian uses for Desolation Island\nmakes it one of his most complex books."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q13). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q014",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q14",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q14",
          "passage": "President Richard Nixon is most famous for his\nparticipation in the 1970s Watergate political\nscandal, a convoluted tale of criminality and\neraded ethics involving a constellation of\nassociates such as Chief of Staff H.R. \"Bob\"\nHaldeman and Nixon's personal friend Charles\n\"Bebe\" Rebozo. But Nixon's legacy is complex:\nhe has been praised for his role in opening\nrelations between the United States and China,\nand he once made an attempt at reforming\nUnited States health care policy that is arguably\na precursor to the Affordable Care Act, which\nbecame law during the Barack Obama\nadministration.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "It catalogs the most notable achievements\nof a historical figure.",
            "It argues that the seeming prominence of a\nhistorical figure is largely due to the\nactions of others.",
            "It assesses a historical figure's significance\nby considering both his achievements and\nfailings.",
            "It explains how a historical figure's\nreputation has improved significantly with\nthe passage of time."
          ],
          "answer": 2,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is C (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q14). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q015",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q15",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q15",
          "passage": "Thermal technologies have been used in the food\nindustry for over 100 years, providing a way of\npreserving food in large batches. Recent\nadvancements in ohmic heating were made\nthrough research in Chile on the preservation of\nstrawberries. Ohmic heating is generally\nconsidered to be an improvement over more\nconventional thermal preservation methods:\nwhereas conventional methods transfer energy\nfrom the surface of a food to its interior, ohmic\nheating passes electric current through food\nproducts to generate heat within the food itself.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "It provides an overview of the research\ninto ohmic heating both globally and in a\nspecific country.",
            "It introduces ohmic heating as a food\npreservation technology and identifies one\nof its advantages.",
            "It explains why conventional heating\nmethods are popular in commercial\napplications and proposes a shift away\nfrom these methods.",
            "It suggests that there are possible flaws in\nohmic heating technologies that have not\nyet been well researched."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q15). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q016",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q16",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q16",
          "passage": "In 2020, rap artist and professor A.D. Carson\npublished the first peer-reviewed rap album\nabout his experiences with Black masculinity\ncalled \"i used to love to dream.\" Typically in\npeer review, experts evaluate scholarly articles\nprior to publication. For Carson's album,\ndubbed a \"mixtap/e/ssay,\" peer review involved\nboth scholars and rap artists. In combining\nelements of a mixtape album with scholarly\nessays that connect Carson's lyrics to historical\nand contemporary contexts for listeners both\ninside and outside academia, Carson's album\nhelped redefine how scholarship is created and\nshared.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To compare the relative public impact of\nscholarly articles and albums.",
            "To describe how each step of the peer\nreview process unfolds.",
            "To capture one scholar's opinion of a new\nrap album.",
            "To explain why a certain rap album is\nparticularly innovative."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q16). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q017",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q17",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q17",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Adib\nKhorram's 2018 novel Darius the Great Is Not\nOkay. Darius, a teenager from the United\nStates, is visiting his grandparents, Babou and\nMamou, in Iran. They are preparing for a\nholiday celebration.\nI felt like a tourist. But Babou fussed with my\ncap a little more, even though Mamou had\nalready gotten it settled. He looked me in the\neye from time to time, like he was looking for\nsomething, and thought maybe just maybe I had\nit in me after all. Babou hummed to himself as\nhe smoothed out my shoulder seams and rested\nhis hands on them. \"I am glad you are here to\nsee this, Darioush-jan.\" Maybe I wasn't such a\ntourist.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To demonstrate that Babou and Darius\naren't very interested in going to new\nplaces.",
            "To emphasize that Darius has been\nlooking forward to an event.",
            "To suggest that Babou is planning to visit\nDarius in the United States soon.",
            "To show that Babou affects the way\nDarius thinks of himself."
          ],
          "answer": 3,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is D (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q17). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t08-q018",
          "skill": "Main Purpose",
          "domain": "Craft and Structure",
          "focus": "Analyze the primary purpose or rhetorical intent of the text as a whole",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 8 · Q18",
          "passageTitle": "Main Purpose — Q18",
          "passage": "In 2011 Brynn M. Devine and colleagues\npublished a study concluding that ocean\nacidification has a strong effect on the behavior\nof Cheilodipterus quinquelineatus, a species of\nfish. However, Devine and colleagues' study\nrelied on a mean sample size of only about 25\nfish. In a 2022 review of various scientists'\nconclusions about the impacts of ocean\nacidification on fish behavior, Timothy D. Clark\nand colleagues caution that relying on such a\nrelatively small sample size can increase the\npotential for biased analysis. Such analysis, in\nturn, can contribute to reports of exaggerated\neffects.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best states the main purpose of\nthe text?",
          "options": [
            "To note a potential concern about the\nfindings of a scientific study.",
            "To discuss an aspect of ocean acidification\nthat is frequently overlooked.",
            "To present a debate between two research\nteams about a cause of ocean acidification.",
            "To explain how the behavior of a fish\nspecies has changed over time."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is A (Answer Key: Topic 8, Q18). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
